.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Source Map JS"
.PP
\fR\fP
.PP
Difference between original \fRsource-map\fP:

.PP
.RS 4
TL,DR: it's fork of original source-map@0\&.6, but with perfomance optimizations\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
This journey starts from \fRsource-map@0\&.7\&.0\fP\&. Some part of it was rewritten to Rust and WASM and API became async\&.

.PP
It's still a major block for many libraries like PostCSS or Sass for example because they need to migrate the whole API to the async way\&. This is the reason why 0\&.6\&.1 has 2x more downloads than 0\&.7\&.3 while it's faster several times\&.

.PP

.PP
More important that WASM version has some optimizations in JS code too\&. This is why \fRcommunity asked to create branch for 0\&.6 version\fP and port these optimizations but, sadly, the answer was «no»\&. A bit later I discovered \fRthe issue\fP created by \fRBen Rothman (@benthemonkey)\fP with no response at all\&.

.PP
\fRRoman Dvornov (@lahmatiy)\fP wrote a \fRserveral posts\fP (russian, only, sorry) about source-map library in his own Telegram channel\&. He mentioned the article \fR«Maybe you don't need Rust and WASM to speed up your JS»\fP written by \fRVyacheslav Egorov (@mraleph)\fP\&. This article contains optimizations and hacks that lead to almost the same performance compare to WASM implementation\&.

.PP
I decided to fork the original source-map and port these optimizations from the article and several others PR from the original source-map\&.

.PP
.PP

.PP
This is a library to generate and consume the source map format \fRdescribed here\fP\&.
.SH "Use with Node"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install source-map-js
.fi
.PP

.PP
